<h2>7. Examples: Real-World Validation Scenarios</h2>

<h3>Scenario 1: E-commerce product page with missing price.</h3>
<p>A major retailer deployed a template update that accidentally removed the price field from the Offer block on 12,000 product pages. The JSON-LD looked like this:</p>
<pre><code>&#123;
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Product",
  "name": "Wireless Bluetooth Headphones",
  "offers": &#123;
    "@type": "Offer",
    "priceCurrency": "USD",
    "availability": "https://schema.org/InStock"
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
<p>The price field is required for Offer. Without it, Google cannot display price-rich snippets. <code>/v1/seo/schema-validate</code> checks top-level required fields (Product needs name), so a nested rule like this one belongs in your own checks, such as the extra_checks function above. The fix was a one-line template change, followed by re-validation of all 12,000 pages.</p>

<h3>Scenario 2: FAQ page with invalid mainEntity structure.</h3>
<p>A SaaS company added FAQ schema to their pricing page but used an incorrect nesting pattern:</p>
<pre><code>&#123;
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    &#123;
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is your pricing model?",
      "acceptedAnswer": "We charge per seat per month."
    &#125;
  ]
&#125;</code></pre>
<p>The acceptedAnswer property must be an Answer object, not a plain string. The corrected version:</p>
<pre><code>&#123;
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    &#123;
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is your pricing model?",
      "acceptedAnswer": &#123;
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "We charge per seat per month."
      &#125;
    &#125;
  ]
&#125;</code></pre>
<p>This is one of the most common FAQPage mistakes.</p>

<h3>Scenario 3: Article page with stale dateModified.</h3>
<p>A news publisher had 8,000 articles with dateModified set to the article's original publish date, even though many had been updated multiple times. AI crawlers use dateModified as a freshness signal. <code>/v1/aeo/freshness-signal</code>, which compares dateModified with the page's other date signals, is a quick way to spot this. The publisher updated their CMS to set dateModified to the actual last edit timestamp, and within two weeks saw articles with accurate timestamps appear in AI Overviews roughly 2.4x more frequently.</p>

<h3>Scenario 4: Organization schema with no sameAs.</h3>
<p>A B2B company had Organization schema on their homepage but no sameAs URLs. AI crawlers use sameAs to link entities across the knowledge graph. A sameAs check in the team's own pipeline flagged it. Adding three sameAs URLs resolved it. The company reported that within three weeks, their brand entity appeared in 40% more AI-generated answers across ChatGPT and Perplexity.</p>

<h2>8. Performance &amp; Benchmarks: What Validating 50,000 Pages Takes</h2>

<p>Every URL call fetches the page, so validation time depends mostly on how fast your own pages respond. Measure it on your own sitemap: run a sample of a few hundred URLs at a fixed concurrency, record p50 and p95 latency and the failure rate, then extrapolate.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Throughput:</strong> total time is roughly URL count × median latency ÷ concurrency. Keep concurrency modest so you do not overload your own origin.</li>
  <li><strong>Detection coverage:</strong> seed a handful of pages with known errors (broken JSON, a missing required field, a lowercase <code>@type</code>) and confirm each one shows up. <code>/v1/seo/schema-validate</code> covers JSON syntax and top-level required fields; nested rules need your own checks.</li>
  <li><strong>Comparison with manual validation:</strong> pasting a URL into Google's Rich Results Test, waiting, and reading the result takes around 20 seconds per URL.</li>
  <li><strong>Cost comparison:</strong> manual validation of 50,000 URLs at 22 seconds each would take about 305 person-hours. At $75/hour, that is $22,875. With the API, each validation is 1 credit, so 50,000 URLs is 50,000 credits; failed calls are refunded.</li>
</ul>

