<h2>15. FAQs</h2>

<h3>Q1. What is a Schema Markup Validator API?</h3>
<p>A web service that programmatically checks JSON-LD, Microdata, and RDFa for syntax correctness, schema.org conformance, Google rich result eligibility, and AI-crawler readiness. Instead of pasting URLs into a browser tool one at a time, you send URLs or raw JSON-LD via HTTP requests and receive structured validation reports. Think of it as a linter for your structured data.</p>

<h3>Q2. How is this different from Google's Rich Results Test?</h3>

<p>Google's Rich Results Test is a browser-based tool for single-URL debugging. It has no public API, no batch processing, and no AI-readiness checks. Ollagraph's <code>/v1/seo/schema-validate</code> is a plain HTTPS endpoint built for automation: loop it over a sitemap, call it from CI/CD, or run it on a schedule, and pair it with <code>/v1/aeo/schema-coverage</code> for an AEO coverage score. If you manage more than 50 pages, the browser tool is costing you time and letting regressions through.</p>

<h3>Q3. Can it validate pages behind a login?</h3>

<p>Not by URL. The validator only fetches public pages and cannot log in or send cookies or custom headers. For pages behind authentication, fetch the HTML yourself and send it in the <code>html</code> field; validation runs the same way.</p>

<h3>Q4. Does it check for Google-specific rich result requirements?</h3>

<p>Yes, for top-level required fields. The validator checks each JSON-LD node against required-field lists for 20+ rich-result types, including Product, Review, FAQPage, HowTo, Recipe, Event and JobPosting, and returns the types the page is eligible for. Nested rules, such as an Offer's <code>price</code>, are best added in your own code.</p>

<h3>Q5. How do I check AI-readiness?</h3>

<p><code>/v1/aeo/schema-coverage</code> scores a page from 0 to 100 against the schema types that matter most for AI answers and lists which are present and missing. <code>/v1/aeo/freshness-signal</code> checks <code>dateModified</code> and the page's other date signals. Checks such as stable <code>@id</code> identifiers and <code>sameAs</code> coverage are a few lines of your own code on the JSON-LD returned by <code>/v1/extract/structured</code>.</p>

<h3>Q6. How many URLs can I validate in a single batch?</h3>

<p>Each call validates one URL or one HTML document. To validate a sitemap, loop over its URLs in your own code with modest concurrency; each call costs 1 credit.</p>

<h3>Q7. What happens if a page has multiple JSON-LD blocks?</h3>

<p>The validator processes every JSON-LD block, and every node inside an array or <code>@graph</code>, independently. Each one gets its own entry, identified by <code>block_index</code> and <code>node_index</code>.</p>

<h3>Q8. How often should I run schema validation?</h3>
<p>At minimum, run validation on every deployment and on a daily schedule. For high-traffic sites, run every 6 hours. The most common schema regressions come from CMS template changes, third-party plugin updates, and CDN configuration modifications — none of which are tied to your deployment schedule.</p>

<h3>Q9. Is the validator GDPR compliant?</h3>

<p>Ollagraph does not store page content: HTML and JSON-LD are processed in memory and discarded after the response. The request URL is recorded in your usage log. If your pages put personal data in their schema, review that before you publish it.</p>

<h3>Q10. What schema formats does the validator support?</h3>

<p>JSON-LD only. <code>/v1/seo/schema-validate</code> does not audit Microdata or RDFa yet. <code>/v1/extract/structured</code> returns a page's Microdata and RDFa if you want to inspect them, and JSON-LD is strongly recommended for production.</p>

<h3>Q11. Can I use this in a GitHub Actions workflow?</h3>

<p>Yes. The API is a plain HTTPS POST, so any CI step can call it and fail the build on broken blocks; the GitHub Actions example above does this with curl and jq. The same pattern works for GitLab CI, CircleCI, Jenkins, and Bitbucket Pipelines.</p>

